WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:07.160
Capsaicin, the molecule in chili peppers, carries no heat.

2
00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:08.960
So why does it burn?

3
00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:14.533
Caterina, Julius and colleagues looked for the protein that capsaicin acts on.

4
00:00:15.467 --> 00:00:19.067
They used cells that do not normally respond to capsaicin.

5
00:00:20.017 --> 00:00:26.257
They added pools of cDNA, copies of sensory neuron genes, and watched calcium inside

6
00:00:26.257 --> 00:00:27.257
the cells.

7
00:00:27.717 --> 00:00:30.257
With most pools, capsaicin did nothing.

8
00:00:31.267 --> 00:00:33.307
A positive pool made the trace jump.

9
00:00:34.347 --> 00:00:37.487
Splitting that pool again and again left a single cDNA.

10
00:00:38.567 --> 00:00:41.147
It made a channel now called TRPV1.

11
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.300
Here is the surprise.

12
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:48.100
The same channel also opens with heat above about 43 °C.

13
00:00:50.233 --> 00:00:54.273
That was the cloned rat channel, in a foreign cell, not human perception.

14
00:00:55.383 --> 00:00:57.323
So capsaicin burns without heat.

15
00:00:58.163 --> 00:01:01.483
It excites sensory neurons that noxious heat also excites.

16
00:01:02.433 --> 00:01:07.413
Predict. A chemical opens a channel that cool temperatures also open.

17
00:01:08.273 --> 00:01:09.353
How will it feel on the skin?

18
00:01:15.383 --> 00:01:18.903
Most likely cool, if it mainly drives cold-sensitive neurons.

19
00:01:19.803 --> 00:01:21.163
That chemical is menthol.

20
00:01:22.103 --> 00:01:24.023
Mint can feel cool without the skin cooling.

21
00:01:25.067 --> 00:01:27.507
Its receptor is called TRPM8.

22
00:01:28.427 --> 00:01:29.707
Cool temperatures open it too.

23
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:33.540
But cloning shows only what is enough in a foreign cell.

24
00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:35.580
Does the animal need it?

25
00:01:36.433 --> 00:01:41.573
Back to TRPV1. Caterina and colleagues knocked that gene out in mice.

26
00:01:42.617 --> 00:01:45.457
The mice showed no pain behaviour to capsaicin.

27
00:01:46.237 --> 00:01:49.137
Their detection of painful heat was only partly impaired.

28
00:01:50.167 --> 00:01:53.807
Mechanical responses were normal, and the heat loss was partial.

29
00:01:54.707 --> 00:01:58.827
So other sensors exist, and TRPV1 is not the pain receptor.

30
00:01:59.967 --> 00:02:02.047
That gives the lesson its one idea.

31
00:02:03.067 --> 00:02:04.067
Follow the line.

32
00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:08.827
A sensory neuron's firing says which line is active, and how strongly.

33
00:02:09.727 --> 00:02:11.967
But it does not say for certain what activated it.

34
00:02:12.867 --> 00:02:16.147
The channels in its endings decide what can activate it.

35
00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:20.750
The next two films follow where each line goes, and who turns it up.

36
00:02:21.683 --> 00:02:23.643
So when something burns, ask first.

37
00:02:24.483 --> 00:02:26.863
Which neurons were driven, and what can drive them?
